# A literal brace in a string (#2).
#
# This exists for the formatter, which the `fmt` suite runs over every case:
# `{` opens an interpolation, so reprinting a string that holds one has to
# escape it again. It did not, and `"a {b} c"` came back out as an
# interpolation of a variable named `b` — a program that still compiled and
# quietly did something else. No case had a brace in a string, so nothing
# caught it.
let who: "world"
say "a \{b} c"
say "plain } brace"
say "both \{ and }"
say "interpolated {who}"
say "mixed \{literal} and {who}"
say "json-ish: \{\"k\": [1, 2]}"
